\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Shelah's eventual categoricity conjecture: For every cardinal $\lambda$ there exists a cardinal $\mu(\lambda)$ such that if an AEC K with LS(K)${} \le \lambda$ is categorical in a cardinal above $\mu(\lambda)$ then it is categorical in all cardinals above $\mu(\lambda)$.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics&oldid=1366636767#Model_theory_and_formal_languages}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Shelah's eventual categoricity conjecture: For every cardinal $\lambda$ there exists a cardinal $\mu(\lambda)$ such that if an AEC K with LS(K)${} \le \lambda$ is categorical in a cardinal above $\mu(\lambda)$ then it is categorical in all cardinals above $\mu(\lambda)$.

